##
    The throwaway.

    A single underscore written where a name belongs binds nothing. It is for the places the
    language hands a program a value and the program has no use for it: the element of a loop
    that only counts, an exception handled rather than read, a result worked out on the way to
    something else.

    Writing one says so. An invented name says the opposite — a reader meeting 'unused' has to
    read to the end of the body to find out it was never used, and the compiler, which cannot
    tell the difference either, says nothing.

    Because it binds nothing, several in one body are ordinary and none of them can be read.
    Only the bare underscore: '_count' and '_x' are names like any other.

    samples/negatives/compile/throwaway.pc holds the other side — where a throwaway is refused,
    and what a local nothing reads is told.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.ALoopThatOnlyCounts();
        Program.AnElementNobodyWants();
        Program.AnExceptionHandledRatherThanRead();
        Program.AResultWorkedOutAndDropped();
        Program.SeveralInOneBody();
    end function

    ##
        A range loop counting three times. The counter exists because the loop counts with one,
        not because the body has anything to ask it.
    ##
    function ALoopThatOnlyCounts()
        Console.WriteLine("Three of the same thing:");

        loop for _ = 1 to 3
            Console.WriteLine("  tick");
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        One line per element, whatever the element is. The walk is over the set's length, and
        naming the element would claim the body reads it.
    ##
    function AnElementNobodyWants()
        integer[] numbers = {4, 5, 6};

        Console.WriteLine("One line per element:");

        loop each _ in numbers
            Console.WriteLine("  one");
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        A catch that deals with the exception by its type alone. Which one arrived is settled by
        the clause that took it, so the value itself has nothing left to say.
    ##
    function AnExceptionHandledRatherThanRead()
        Console.WriteLine("Handled by type alone:");

        try
            throw new ArgumentException("thrown on purpose");
        catch ArgumentException _
            Console.WriteLine("  caught, and the type was the whole answer");
        end try
    end function

    ##
        A call made for what it does rather than what it gives back. The initializer still runs:
        a throwaway drops the value, not the work that produced it.
    ##
    function AResultWorkedOutAndDropped()
        Console.WriteLine("Called for its effect:");

        let _ = Program.Announced();

        # A statement drops what it does not use, so writing '_ =' says what was going to
        # happen anyway. The language has an opinion about it, kept here to show the form runs.
        # ignore PC0258
        _ = Program.Announced();
    end function

    ##
        Two throwaways in one body, and a name beginning with an underscore beside them. The
        first two are not a clash, because neither declares anything to clash with. The third
        is an ordinary name and is read like one.
    ##
    function SeveralInOneBody()
        let _ = 1;
        integer _ = 2;
        integer _count = 3;

        Console.WriteLine("An ordinary name beginning with '_': " + _count);
    end function

    integer function Announced()
        Console.WriteLine("  worked it out");
        yield 7;
    end function
end model
